WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.360
ALLAN LIVINGSTON:
Today Rishi and I

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:14.490
are going to run you through how
to surface data driven insights

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.610
from the G Suite admin platform.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.200
And also tying in with BigQuery.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.329
So we're going to
see if we can--

00:00:24.329 --> 00:00:25.370
I thought we were sorted.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.670
If we can get the slides up.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.360
Let me first give
you some background

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.659
on why this is important.

00:00:31.659 --> 00:00:32.159
Right?

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:36.400
So you saw today at the
keynote a lot of the speakers

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.430
talking about
moving to the cloud,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.780
and how there's so much more
data now that's in the cloud.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.390
And it's great that
there's data in the cloud.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.900
But it's only really useful if
you can get insights from it

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.070
and if it is actionable.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.580
And that's what we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.174
Specifically, we're going
to zoom in on security.

00:00:58.174 --> 00:01:00.090
We're going to talk about
some other elements,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.939
but I think security
is a key piece.

00:01:01.939 --> 00:01:03.480
Because if you really
think about it,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.130
that's very much what is
on CIOs' minds these days.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:17.040
And so one of the things that
having detailed reporting does,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.250
is it allows you to move from
a standardized security posture

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.770
to ones tailored for
your organization.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.170
So if you think about a
standard set of policies

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.230
that you might have, that
might be either too permissive

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.391
or too restrictive
for your organization.

00:01:33.391 --> 00:01:35.640
Or it might both at the same
time for different groups

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.720
of your organization.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.680
By having detailed
reporting and insights,

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.920
you can determine where you
need to tighten the controls

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.840
and where you're OK.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.910
And so we're going to
run you through that.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:55.460
And the way that we do
that is through reporting,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.350
and it allows you to
do a number of things.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:02.620
It allows you to assess
where your risk points are,

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.120
and we're going to show that
in a few different elements.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.854
And also, very much
importantly, it

00:02:06.854 --> 00:02:08.479
allows you to make
timely intervention.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.362
So when you detect
there's a problem,

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:11.820
you need to be able
to act on that.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.320
You need to be able to
do something specific.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.370
You can set security controls.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.760
You can also set up
DLP, which allows

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.231
you to control your sharing
policy based on content

00:02:22.231 --> 00:02:23.980
and based on teams and
things like-- we're

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.510
going to go back that.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.030
And audit allows you to
understand ongoing incidents

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.850
and respond to them
really, really quickly.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:39.250
And finally, we look
outside security.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.840
The other piece
from reporting is

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.520
to really understand
how organizations

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.970
are adopting the G Suite.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:44.520
Right?

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.240
When you saw in
there-- the keynote,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.090
everyone's talking about
how the G Suite transforms

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:49.840
your organization.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.840
Well, it only really
transform your organization

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.780
if you use it.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.940
And what we found at
a lot of customers

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.640
is that you'll have certain
pockets where they're really

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.880
using a lot of collaboration
and some other pockets

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:01.820
where they're not.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.950
And a lot of times
that indicates

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.750
that there may be some
trainings or some warnings

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.550
need to flow from one
area to the other,

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.620
so that your whole
organization is really

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.720
taking advantage of all
the benefits of going

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.760
[INAUDIBLE] with the G Suite.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.300
And so there are
three main elements

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.810
to our reporting platform.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.800
It's got comprehensive data.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.580
What I mean by that is, you
want to go deep and wide

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.029
across the G Suite
products there.

00:03:30.029 --> 00:03:32.570
We've got all the logos, or many
of the logos of the G Suite,

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.900
and we continue to add more.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.750
And you go wide by
covering all the reports

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.460
and then having a lot
of detail reports.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:44.150
We've got 55 usage reports
on the Admin console, 9 audit

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.950
streams, and 18 alerts.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:48.510
And all of this is
really, really increasing.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.350
And so that provides
your baseline,

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:52.460
your key pieces there.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:56.090
And then we want to have
an extensible platform.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.640
APIs that allows us to push the
data out to where you need it.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.160
OK?

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.360
So partners can
integrate with the APIs

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.250
to customized solutions, and
we can also send the data over

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:10.070
to BigQuery and run some really,
really interesting analysis.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.450
And we're going to
show that today.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.530
And then finally, all of
that-- you're doing all of that

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.390
not just so that there's data.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.696
You're doing it so that
you can get insights,

00:04:19.696 --> 00:04:21.320
and that you can act
on those insights.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.110
And we're going to show you
how the reporting system ties

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:24.710
into that.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.170
You can develop insights,
you can surface them,

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.188
and then you can, most crucially
as I've said, act on them.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.330
So there are three
particular types

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.877
of reports that a reporting
platform provides.

00:04:39.877 --> 00:04:41.335
We've got domain
and usage reports.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:48.350
We've got over 100 metrics in
the reporting API in this area.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:49.460
We've also got audit.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.310
And that's about getting near
real time updates on user

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.730
activity and across all
the core G Suite apps

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.710
that I talked about.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.620
The final piece is alerts.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.870
Admins can choose from
two predefined alerts

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.690
to create custom alerts, and
they also create custom alerts

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:08.680
from the audit stream.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.290
And then developers
can push alerts

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.350
in through the reporting API.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:17.440
Rishi is going to give us
an overview of how reporting

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.470
is set up in the admin console.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.122
RISHI DHAND: Thanks, Allan.

00:05:22.122 --> 00:05:23.080
Good evening, everyone.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.720
So I'm going to talk briefly
about all our reporting

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.630
capabilities that we have
today on the admin console.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.880
The first important thing
that I want to talk about

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.430
is on the domain level.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.609
So admins have this
requirement to see

00:05:35.609 --> 00:05:38.150
at a domain level, what are the
top things that are going on.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.990
So our domain level reports
are split into categories.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.990
The first section being
the highlights section.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.330
We realized that admins
are very busy people

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.550
and they need a quick
snapshot of what's

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.180
happening inside the company.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.290
So the highlights page
provides a 360 degree view

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.150
of all the important
things that are

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.391
happening inside the domain.

00:05:56.391 --> 00:05:56.890
Right?

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:59.770
From, what's the seven
day active usage, what's

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.690
the file sharing status
inside the company,

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.580
are there any users
who have suspended,

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.122
are there any users
who are blocked,

00:06:07.122 --> 00:06:09.080
are there any users who
are nearing the quota--

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.590
and that's a clue for admin
to go and purchase more quota.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.100
So these are really
important inputs

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.860
that an admin can get
a quick glance off.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.070
But that's only good for
getting a quick snapshot.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.530
What if an admin wants
to get a more deep dive

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.840
information into each of
these different applications?

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.840
So for that we have
aggregate reports.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.930
Aggregate reports provide,
at a domain level,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.540
data on each of the services.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.310
So for example, if you
want to know how many files

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.600
exist inside your company?

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.870
How many files are shared
outside the domain?

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.202
How many encrypted emails
exist in your company?

00:06:45.202 --> 00:06:47.410
In a world where mobile
device management is becoming

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:48.880
very important,
admins would like

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.700
to know how many
manage devices exist

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.600
inside the company, broken down
by iOS, Android, and so on.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.180
So aggregate reports provides
that level of information

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:02.200
where admins can go deep dive
into each of the services,

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.320
each of the device
management attributes,

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.750
and even export this
information into a Google

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.710
spreadsheet or a CSV and
do further drill downs.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.890
The next category that I
want to talk about is--

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:16.640
we have spoken about
domain level reports.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.040
But what if admin wants to
drill down at a user level?

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.950
For example, if you
as an admin want

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.810
to roll out two-step
verification

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.540
inside a company, which is a
very powerful way to secure

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:27.380
it account.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.320
At that stage you want to
know which users are enrolled

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.350
in two-step verification,
which users are not enrolled,

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.000
and for those who
are not enrolled,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.080
you need to inform them.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.940
And that's where the user level
reports become very important.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:42.620
They provide you granular
data at each user level.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.290
And we provide three different
views around user level data.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.050
So you could have
a security view,

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:51.391
where you can slice and dice
by all the security related

00:07:51.391 --> 00:07:51.890
metrics.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.520
For example, you want to know
how many external applications

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.500
have my users installed.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.870
Given that there's
a lot of sensitivity

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.760
around external
third party data.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.600
So that's a very
important attribute.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.500
The second one could be how
many users have enrolled

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.850
in two-step verification.

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:10.770
The third one could be, are
they installing less secure

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:11.870
applications or not?

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:13.730
Are they using security keys?

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.510
Are they sharing files
outside the domain?

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.390
So that's the security view.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.060
You could also have pure
collaboration and adoption

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.680
view of user level reports
just to discover how many users

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.390
are actively creating docs?

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.640
How many users are
sharing these files?

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.989
Because the company
has gone Google, not

00:08:31.989 --> 00:08:34.280
just for cost savings, but
also to bring about a change

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.650
in the collaborative culture.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.118
And that report
helps you discover

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.909
what are the collaboration
trends happening

00:08:40.909 --> 00:08:41.750
inside the company?

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.390
Are there specific users
who are not using docs

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.070
as much as other users?

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.570
And that's a clue for an
admin to basically go in,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:51.810
conduct maybe some
trainings for these users.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.960
So that's what makes user level
sports very, very important.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:58.520
And I want to highlight
that these reports offer

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.870
a lot of flexibility.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.390
You can download these reports
in a Google spreadsheet

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:02.920
or a CSV.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:06.650
We can do advanced
filtering, you

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.740
can do sorting on
top of this data.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.740
So there's a lot of
flexibility that is offered.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:16.670
The next one that I want to
really highlight is audit.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:18.374
All the reports
are great, but what

00:09:18.374 --> 00:09:19.790
if I want to know
what's happening

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.620
right now inside my company?

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.300
What if I want to
get a real time

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.720
update into what files are
being shared outside my company?

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:29.750
What if I want to know what
third party apps are getting

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:31.070
installed inside my company?

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.420
That's where audit comes in.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.330
Audit is the backbone of all
security and investigation

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:36.050
work.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.210
And we have nine
audit streams that are

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.060
available on Admin Console,
which cover all the major core

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.720
G Suite applications, Gmail
Drive, Calendar, Groups.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.780
And you get a real time feed
into every single activity.

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:50.360
Now imagine the scale
of this feature.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.100
Every single event that the user
is doing inside the company,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:53.600
right?

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.530
From viewing a file to sharing
a file to downloading a file,

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.150
is all captured.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.970
To give you some stats, we
process almost a billion events

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:04.220
a day, just on Drive audit.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.110
That's the scale that
we are operating at.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.510
And these events are
used by our admins

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.180
to troubleshoot when a
security incident has happened.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.680
These events are
used by partners

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.230
to build really advanced
tools on top of it.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.930
So it's really a backbone
of all the security

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:21.640
related investigation
work that happens.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:24.600
I also want to highlight--
we also have a device audit.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.500
So you can now see
every time a device gets

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.230
routed, every time there's
an OS update on the device.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.230
So you can get a real
time feed on that as well.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:39.080
The next vertical
here is alerting.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.370
We realized that admins
are very busy people.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.750
They cannot come to reports and
keep checking what's happening.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.340
That's where alerts come in.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.620
We've offered a set
and forget experience

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.020
where admins can simply
come, choose from one

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:52.850
of the 18 predefined alerts--

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:54.530
for example, suspicious log in.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.670
Just go and turn
on suspicious log

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:58.250
in on your manage
alerts section.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:01.880
And you will know whenever we
detect any suspicious activity.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.960
And we apply a wide range
of intelligence signals

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.510
to detect whether there is
a suspected hijacker who

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:10.520
has come into your domain.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:12.020
So we use that information.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:15.200
You could set alert on, say,
a change of Drive settings.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:16.640
That's very
important, especially

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:19.250
if you have delegated
super-admin responsibilities

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:21.380
to other delegated
admins, and you

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:24.170
want to keep a tab on what
the other delegated admins are

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:24.830
doing.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:27.175
So predefined alerts
are a great way

00:11:27.175 --> 00:11:29.300
to be aware of what's
happening inside your company

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:33.320
and make timely intervention,
avoid any security breaches

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:34.910
to happen.

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:37.460
And if you have a very
custom requirement where

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.510
you want to track a specific
event or a specific file,

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:42.710
that is also possible.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:45.140
So you can go to Drive audit,
or any of the other audit

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.230
streams, do the filtering
on the left side,

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.820
and then set an
alert on top of it.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.486
That's where your
custom alerts come in.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.210
I also want to talk a little
bit about the reports API.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.610
Now, all of the stuff
that we just spoke about

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.250
is great on the Admin Console.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:03.990
You can come run your
reports, you can do audit,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:04.980
you can do alerts.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.110
But what if I have a
very specific use case

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.910
and that my company
needs to achieve?

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.470
What if my CIO has
come to me and said,

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.210
create a collaboration
dashboard for our company.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:16.200
What if my CIO has
come and told me that,

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.794
OK, create a security
dashboard for my company.

00:12:18.794 --> 00:12:20.460
This is where it's
very important for us

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.760
to give all of this data to you,
which you can use and derive

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.770
programmatically, and
build whatever you

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.510
want to build for your company.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.300
Or have this API
given to partners.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.790
And we have a lot of talented
partners on our Google Apps

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.580
Marketplace who have done
great things with the reporting

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.030
and audit API.

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:41.430
And build advance use cases
to meet our large customers.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.550
So reporting API is
a powerful mechanism

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.406
to get over 100 metrics.

00:12:47.406 --> 00:12:48.780
And through push
notification you

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:52.290
can create workflows built
on this particular API.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.190
We have about two0 plus
partner integrations,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.630
and I'll briefly talk about
one such integration, which

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:58.590
is Better Cloud.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.470
So they have done a good
job of using the reporting

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.590
API to build a two-step
verification reporting

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:05.520
interface.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.920
They also have an apps
explorer interface

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.300
where you can discover third
party apps or track quota

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:10.800
users.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.050
So this is just one example.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.360
There are a lot of other
partners on Google Apps

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.260
Marketplace who have
integrated this API,

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.660
and it's helping them
meet sophisticated use

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:21.030
cases, like DLP, custom
workflows, and so on.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:25.690
But this I'll hand
it back to Allan.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:26.030
ALLAN LIVINGSTON:
Thank you, Rishi.

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:26.690
So, yeah.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.710
So there is an overview
of admin reporting.

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.770
But let's try to put
this into context

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.900
with some specific examples.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:41.600
And so as admins, we
hear two general classes

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.616
of requests from CIOs, CEOs,
chief security officers.

00:13:46.616 --> 00:13:47.990
It's basically,
you've got how do

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:51.960
I secure my users, my
domain, my company?

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:54.150
So you've got security
in risk management.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:58.817
And then, how do I
ensure that my users are

00:13:58.817 --> 00:13:59.900
as productive as possible?

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:02.540
That I'm actually
transforming my culture

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:03.620
by moving to the cloud?

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.835
And that's when we come into
uses in adoption reporting.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:16.140
And to do this we can
use our audit logs.

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:20.100
Now the audit logs provide
near real time access

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.810
to events that are occurring.

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.090
So we can use those to
detect what's going on,

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.800
and then we can set
appropriate policies and rules

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.660
to protect the
data in the domain.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:34.110
Now admins can slice
and dice audit data

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:36.300
in a number of different
ways, and we're

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.550
going to go over that now.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.430
And so things like--

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:45.120
we've got here how to track
external file sharing.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:46.620
So this is something
that we've seen

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:50.490
that's come up an awful
lot as companies have moved

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:54.000
to the cloud more and
increasingly put more and more

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.740
sensitive data into Drive.

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:57.990
One of the first things that--

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.870
first questions
that we got was, how

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.561
do I know that my data is safe?

00:15:02.561 --> 00:15:03.810
How do I know what's going on?

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.400
Maybe someone could just
be sharing it everywhere.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.170
Before, they had
that sense of control

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:09.560
when they had it on premise.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.070
And when they move
it to the cloud,

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:12.780
you lose that sense
of control, and you

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.359
want to understand what's
going on with your data.

00:15:15.359 --> 00:15:16.650
And that's where this comes in.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.860
So Rishi is going to
show us a quick demo

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.780
on how you can detect this
and what kind of actions

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:22.790
you should take.

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:23.656
Thank you, Rishi.

00:15:23.656 --> 00:15:24.781
RISHI DHAND: Thanks, Allan.

00:15:24.781 --> 00:15:28.006
So as Allan just mentioned,
file security around Drive files

00:15:28.006 --> 00:15:29.130
is really, really critical.

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:31.280
And we have built some
really advanced tools

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.990
that I would like to
showcase today and see

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:37.090
how admins can protect their
data using these tools.

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:43.620
So let's say, I'm a
user and my manager

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.960
shared this sales
forecast document with me

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.270
and said, hey, do some
additional enhancements

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:49.249
to this file.

00:15:49.249 --> 00:15:50.790
And I want to work
on this from home,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.580
and my company has a
secured access from home.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:16:00.110
So what I'll do is,
I share this file,

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:02.060
and I say, anyone with a link--

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:04.710
so that I can go back
home and do this--

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.760
can edit.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:08.590
And save.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:12.100
So I haven't put my [INAUDIBLE]
I'm being very smart here.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:15.460
So my name doesn't show
up in the sharing list.

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:18.680
And then, let's see
what the admin sees.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:20.182
Now I'm the admin.

00:16:20.182 --> 00:16:21.640
I'm going to the
reporting section.

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:34.130
And I will check Drive audit.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.130
So there you go.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:44.160
I just have got an
alert over here saying,

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:46.590
confidential sales
plan has been shared.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:57.640
And when I go to my email
I've also got an email saying,

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.474
this user has changed the link
sharing visibility from private

00:17:00.474 --> 00:17:01.390
to anyone with a link.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.227
Now, this is a red alert
for me as an admin.

00:17:03.227 --> 00:17:04.810
I have a user who
has shared something

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.089
confidential to
anyone with a link.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:10.510
And this can lead to large
scale security breaches,

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:13.300
because a link is
almost like a password,

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.579
so anyone who has a link
can go into the file.

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:22.940
So I go back to my admin
console, I click on the file,

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:29.264
and first and foremost, I can
go back and change this option

00:17:29.264 --> 00:17:30.430
so that this file is secure.

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:36.090
Now I've done this, but
this is a reactive step.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:38.040
I can't do this for
every single file.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.110
So what is the
solution as an admin

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.490
that I can get my mental peace,
and set policies and control

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:47.070
so that my users don't do
these accidental mistakes.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:47.630
Right?

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:51.300
Like they don't put the
security of the domain address.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:18:00.960
So I would like to show you
that we can now create rules.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.430
So I go to the admin console.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.260
And this a our
brand new Drive DLP

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:06.840
feature that we have
recently announced.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.950
And as an admin, I have a list
of pre-defined templates--

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.380
Drive data loss
prevention templates--

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:14.760
that I can choose from.

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.680
Or I can create from
a blank template.

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.930
So I select one of them.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.400
I can rename.

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:32.684
And I can do a host
of other things here.

00:18:32.684 --> 00:18:34.850
While I'm creating this
rule, I can either assign it

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:36.680
to an organization unit.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:39.590
Or, even better, I
can use Google Groups.

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:42.355
I can assign it to a bunch of
users inside Google Groups.

00:18:42.355 --> 00:18:44.480
We have heard this request
from a lot of customers,

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:46.564
that they would like to
see more and more policies

00:18:46.564 --> 00:18:48.563
in the settings being
assigned to Google Groups.

00:18:48.563 --> 00:18:50.930
So this is one such example
where we have done that.

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.470
And on the content side,
with a predefined template

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:55.760
I get three templates.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:01.310
But I can customize this
and add more out of one--

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:06.230
and pick one out of 50
such predefined templates.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:08.020
Say I choose credit card number.

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.920
And the third field here
is very interesting.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:13.910
Drive DLP does
flagging of files.

00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:16.450
And if I want to be
very, very precise

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:18.370
and avoid any false
positives, I will

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:21.670
choose a very high confidence
threshold, which not only looks

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.200
at in case of
credit card-- number

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:24.908
not only looks at the
credit card number,

00:19:24.908 --> 00:19:27.130
but it also looks at some
keywords around the number,

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:29.590
say expiry or name.

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:31.490
And it does that
kind of a matching.

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:35.860
So I do that and
I'm done with this.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:39.850
And then within the actions
I can not only notify,

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:42.550
which I was able to do
with my custom alerts,

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:45.700
but I can actually block
external access, which

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.160
is a very powerful way
to secure your data based

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:50.170
on content scanning.

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:51.160
So I've done this.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:54.160
I've created and
activated this rule.

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:59.990
And let's see what
happens on the user side.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.330
So now I have another
doc and I'm Rio,

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:08.400
and I've been asked to work on
some of the sensitive team data

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.470
and I try to do the same thing.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:17.010
And I'm trying to share it
with, say a consumer ID.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:18.080
And there you go.

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:20.700
I get an error saying, this item
contains credit card number,

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:21.671
and you cannot share.

00:20:21.671 --> 00:20:22.170
Right?

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.210
So this is a great
example of how

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:27.930
admins can protect sensitive
data stored inside Drive files,

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:32.850
and also audit it when
such situations happen.

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:35.070
So I just want to highlight
another thing here.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:46.030
As an admin you can go back to
your DLP interface and check

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:47.410
rules audit.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.390
So you will get a
list of all the files

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:54.040
that are matching your DLP
rules and run detail drill downs

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:55.990
on top of this.

00:20:55.990 --> 00:21:05.092
With this I will go back to our
presentation to our next topic.

00:21:05.092 --> 00:21:06.550
ALLAN LIVINGSTON:
Thank you, Rishi.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:09.880
So shows you a
really powerful way

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:16.619
to detect and then control
the sharing of Drive files.

00:21:16.619 --> 00:21:18.910
And it's really powerful but
quick, and that's the key.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.070
That was the design
principle that we

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:23.140
had when we thought about DLP.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.840
We wanted to make it not
cumbersome, really automatic.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.550
Give users a good experience and
give admins a good experience.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:31.120
And that's the idea with all
of our security features.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:35.200
And so every time, as
the cloud continues

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:38.620
to get more and more powerful
and you add new capabilities,

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.750
you also have to
secure it in new ways.

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:45.250
And third party data
access is a great example.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.130
So third party apps
are really powerful.

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:49.840
You can integrate with
G Suite using our API.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.236
And you can extend the
capabilities of the G Suite

00:21:52.236 --> 00:21:55.210
and integrate G Suite data
with your wider workflows.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.250
So that's a great capability.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:21:59.350
But on the other
hand, we've also

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:02.500
got to ensure that we're
adequately monitoring

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.910
and controlling that.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:10.500
And that's where access to third
parties and logging in controls

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:12.150
around that come into play.

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.400
And so Rishi is going to
run us through a demo of how

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:15.150
you would do that.

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.460
How you would first
detect the behavior

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:19.230
you're seeing in third
party applications.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.490
Is it what you expect
your user to be doing?

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.170
Are they doing this
responsibly or not?

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:25.240
And then how you
can control that.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.380
So for Rishi, if you can
go to the demo, please.

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:28.505
RISHI DHAND: Thanks, Allan.

00:22:28.505 --> 00:22:30.630
So I'll quickly show
how, as an admin,

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:33.450
I can track all the third
party apps that are currently

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:35.320
being used inside my company.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:40.350
So I'm back to the
admin console and I

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.850
go to my reporting section.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:46.690
And I go to the user security
report, which I spoke about.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:52.420
And this report has a column
called external application.

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:55.090
So I choose that, and I
say, greater than zero,

00:22:55.090 --> 00:22:58.760
just to scan all the users who
have installed applications.

00:22:58.760 --> 00:22:59.830
So there you go.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:01.660
It has very quickly
told me, these

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.160
are the users
inside your company

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:04.670
who are using
external applications.

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:06.910
So I click on one of them.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:07.930
Our favorite, Rio.

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:16.110
And we go back to his security
section inside reals page

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.134
and there you go.

00:23:17.134 --> 00:23:19.050
So I see he has installed
a particular thought

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:22.330
by the application I
see this app and I say,

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:25.050
OK that's not in my approved
list, so I said, revoke.

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:26.010
And I say, revoke.

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:27.600
Now again, this is
an example where

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:30.390
I have taken a reactive step.

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.580
My user has gone
and installed it,

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:34.320
and I have gone and revoked it.

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:35.830
This is not scalable.

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:38.490
We want to give admins control
where they can set policies,

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:41.340
so that users know
which apps to install

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:42.960
and which apps are
not authorized.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.970
Now let's see how
he can do that.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:52.310
So I'm going to show you a brand
new feature that is currently

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.670
in testing, entrusted
tester to limited customers.

00:23:55.670 --> 00:23:58.500
And there you go.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:01.290
So you have G Suite
API permissions.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:04.970
This is a new section that we
have created which shows you

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:06.932
what scopes are
currently allowed,

00:24:06.932 --> 00:24:08.390
what data permissions
are currently

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:10.010
allowed inside your domain.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:14.870
Now notice that you
can also go and check

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:16.289
on what applications
are currently

00:24:16.289 --> 00:24:17.580
being used inside your company.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:18.350
So there you go.

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:22.220
I'm able to filter by
application scope, what

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.740
apps are being currently
use inside my company.

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:32.610
And I can go back to the
security permissions page

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.360
and block any one of them.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:39.770
So I say, disable contacts,
and it disables contacts.

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:42.370
So from here on,
users will not be

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:45.460
able to use apps that
have access to contacts.

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:49.255
Now if there is a use case
where I want to explicitly allow

00:24:49.255 --> 00:24:51.310
a particular application
inside my company,

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:52.560
that's pretty easy too.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:54.130
I go to my installed
applications,

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:56.620
I open this button,
and I say, trust.

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:59.740
The moment I trust this
application, what it does

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:02.650
is it starts
honoring, or allowing,

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:05.870
any access done by this
particular application.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:09.760
So now admin has created a very
safe playground for his users,

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:12.010
where they are free to go
and use applications that

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:15.120
are authorized, request for
applications that are not there

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:17.000
in the trusted list
from the admin,

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:18.730
and admin can do
his due diligence

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:21.160
and agree to give permission.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:22.960
So that way this
is a good balance

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.924
between not stopping
the innovation that's

00:25:24.924 --> 00:25:27.340
coming through the code by the
apps, but at the same time,

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.520
making sure these
third party apps

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:34.470
are using data in
a secure manner.

00:25:34.470 --> 00:25:36.017
With that I'll hand
it back to Allan.

00:25:36.017 --> 00:25:37.600
I'll just go back
to the presentation.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:38.140
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Great.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:38.920
Thanks, Rishi.

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:41.580
And so that's a really powerful
app, and as Rishi said,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:42.880
a powerful set of capabilities.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:45.580
That ability to balance,
allowing innovation,

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:48.250
giving the users what they want,
but also controlling the way

00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:49.600
data flows.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:51.130
Now let's talk about
another example

00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:54.940
here, which is tracking
suspicious logins

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:56.210
and acting on those.

00:25:56.210 --> 00:26:01.750
And so if you think about
one of your primary risks

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:04.780
you're going to see as
an admin or as a company,

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:07.240
it's compromised accounts.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:08.980
And you know a
lot of simple ways

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:10.480
that accounts are compromised.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:14.410
Things like, just very
wide password reuse,

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:15.970
poor quality passwords.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:19.720
The other thing that's present
and has been, and continues

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:24.550
to be a big problem,
is phishing, basically

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:27.460
fake pages that trick users
into putting in their passwords.

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:30.220
And it's still an
extremely common problem

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.440
and it doesn't look
like user education is

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.330
going to solve that.

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:36.724
And so it's something
that we have

00:26:36.724 --> 00:26:38.140
to be really aware
of that there's

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:41.620
a lot of, either targeted
spear phishing attempts where

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.120
you going to go
after individuals,

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:46.600
or just broad based phishing
is relatively common practice

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:48.360
and relatively successful.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.410
And so obviously
as an admin, you

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:52.540
want to be able to detect
that kind of behavior,

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:55.074
and you want to act on it.

00:26:55.074 --> 00:26:57.490
And we're really excited by
the capabilities in this area.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.670
And so Rishi's going to run
us through a real quick demo

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.570
in that regard, and
show us all the things

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.000
that we're doing there
to prevent hijacking,

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:08.105
which is obviously a huge, huge
problem you're going to see.

00:27:08.105 --> 00:27:09.230
RISHI DHAND: Thanks, Allan.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:12.260
So I'll quickly show you
what tools admins have today

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:15.680
to track suspicious logins, and
then take proactive action just

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:18.560
like how we have seen in
the case of file sharing

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:19.880
and third party apps.

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:21.860
So I go back to my
reporting section

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:24.420
where we have an audit
stream called login audit.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:26.600
Now here you will
see that admins

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:29.630
are able to track in real time
what users are logging in,

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:31.640
from which IP they
are logging in,

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:35.840
and I can also filter
based on a specific IP.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:45.000
So I can copy, paste an IP,
and put it in my IP list,

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.950
and there you go.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:50.360
So I'm basically able
to understand which IP

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:51.700
this particular user is doing.

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:53.366
So now I'm a little
concerned about, OK,

00:27:53.366 --> 00:27:57.280
this IP is unfamiliar, what can
I do to prevent such things.

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:01.120
So I can go back to this
particular user account

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:04.240
and see if he has used two-step
verification, because that's

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:07.570
a very powerful way to
prevent any phishing attempts.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.190
So I see he's not using.

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:19.070
And then I can basically
go back to my report

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:22.290
which shows how many users are
using two-step verification.

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:24.890
So I go back to my
security report,

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:28.580
and I check two-step
verification enrollment.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:32.570
And I say, show me all
the users who are not

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:34.570
using two-step verification.

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:35.510
There you go.

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:38.710
So now it's showing me the
trending of all the users

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:40.250
who are not using,
and in my domain

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:42.740
I only have 8 users
who are enrolled.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.386
So this is a big problem.

00:28:44.386 --> 00:28:46.010
I only have a few
users that I enrolled

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:47.540
in two-step
verification, and I want

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:52.040
to enforce it as a policy
to protect my domain.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:56.390
So I go back to the
security section,

00:28:56.390 --> 00:28:57.855
which has the list of policies.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:04.392
I go to the basic
settings, and I say, OK, I

00:29:04.392 --> 00:29:05.350
want to go and enforce.

00:29:10.164 --> 00:29:10.830
So there you go.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.320
So now I have this
screen where I

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:16.520
can decide to enforce
two-step verification by OU.

00:29:16.520 --> 00:29:19.640
So for example, if I want
to enforce it on my vendors,

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:22.569
I can turn this on,
and I can turn it

00:29:22.569 --> 00:29:24.110
on from a future
date, because I want

00:29:24.110 --> 00:29:26.960
to give some time to
my users to enroll

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:28.337
in two-step verification.

00:29:28.337 --> 00:29:30.920
And this is the new section that
we have recently added, allow

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:32.520
two-step verification methods.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:36.260
Where admins can choose
not only to enforce

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:38.840
two-step verification, but
they can take and bump up

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:42.260
the security further by
enforcing only security keys,

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:45.297
which is the new way to
actually authenticate

00:29:45.297 --> 00:29:46.130
for a second factor.

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.620
Which is a very powerful way
because it uses the hardware.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:53.640
So I can say, only
security keys and save it.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:56.550
So that's how an
admin can protect

00:29:56.550 --> 00:29:58.310
against phishing
attempts by enforcing

00:29:58.310 --> 00:29:59.790
two-step verification.

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:05.440
And with this I will go
back to the presentation.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:06.282
Back to you, Allan.

00:30:06.282 --> 00:30:07.740
ALLAN LIVINGSTON:
Thank you, Rishi.

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:12.310
And that showed an
example of security keys,

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:17.370
which is a really unusual
feature in the security world.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:22.500
Because if we're
honest, most times

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:25.661
enhanced security is kind of
a trade off with usability,

00:30:25.661 --> 00:30:26.160
right?

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:29.520
Putting extra elements
of security in place,

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:30.990
kind of, degrades
user experience

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:32.990
or makes it more of
a hassle for users.

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:36.270
And security key's a
really unusual area,

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:37.740
because it doesn't do that.

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:41.400
It's extremely easy to
use, and it's much easier

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.760
to use than other two-factor
authentication mechanisms.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:47.400
And it's also more
secure, because it's

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.010
unphishable You can still
do sophisticated phishing

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:55.965
accounts on other second
factor log in mechanisms.

00:30:55.965 --> 00:30:57.840
But you can't do that
on security key because

00:30:57.840 --> 00:31:02.430
of the hardware connection to
the computer, which is amazing.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:05.622
And it's also, just as I
said, it's really easy to use.

00:31:05.622 --> 00:31:07.080
We use them all
the time at Google.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:08.730
I think everyone at
Google uses them.

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:12.030
And there's a study up on
the Yubico website, who's

00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:13.830
a manufacturer of these things.

00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:18.450
And it showed that Googlers
actually spent four times less

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:22.020
than using standard
second factor mechanisms

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:23.950
by using these security keys.

00:31:23.950 --> 00:31:26.790
These little-- let me just pull
this out of Rishi's computer--

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:28.220
this little guy right here.

00:31:28.220 --> 00:31:30.990
And you basically just
stick it in your USB port,

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.840
and any time you want two-factor
auth, you just press it.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:37.390
And it's super, super useful.

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:42.270
So let's talk about admins.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:45.480
And if you want, who is
watching the watchers?

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:46.980
How does this work?

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:49.380
And this is
particularly a challenge

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:51.600
for larger organizations
that, a lot of times,

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:53.340
are going to have
delegated admins.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:55.173
You're gonna have a
number delegated admins.

00:31:55.173 --> 00:31:57.720
And it's possible that
delegated admins--

00:31:57.720 --> 00:32:00.240
while delegation is a
really powerful tool,

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:02.130
it allows you to
spread the load.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.819
It's also possible that
delegated admins, perhaps,

00:32:04.819 --> 00:32:06.360
don't have the same
level of training

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:09.960
or the same understanding of
the impact of their actions.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:12.690
And for that we've got to
keep a close eye on what

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:14.950
the admins are up to as well.

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:17.110
So Rishi's gonna show you
a quick example of that.

00:32:17.110 --> 00:32:18.443
RISHI DHAND: Sure, thanks Allan.

00:32:18.443 --> 00:32:20.317
So as Allan just
mentioned, it's very

00:32:20.317 --> 00:32:21.900
important for
super-admins, especially

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:23.358
in large organizations,
where there

00:32:23.358 --> 00:32:25.890
is a hierarchical structure,
a lot of delegated admins,

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:27.840
also super-admins,
to keep tabs on what

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.310
the new super admins are doing.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:33.730
So I'll quickly show you
on how that could work.

00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:37.710
So we have a delegated
admin, Shivani, who's

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:42.850
logging into her admin console.

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:46.150
And she's been given a services
admin role, which basically

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:49.090
means she's in charge of
managing settings for each

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:50.000
of the applications.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:55.560
So I go to my server
settings, I go to my Drive,

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:58.270
and she's been tasked with
reviewing sharing settings

00:32:58.270 --> 00:33:01.090
and taking appropriate
actions there.

00:33:01.090 --> 00:33:08.380
And as I go there, I have
decided to uncheck this,

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:12.160
and by mistake, I also uncheck
another setting that says,

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:15.270
warn the user when you're
sharing outside of the domain.

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:17.850
So I uncheck it, by
mistake, and I save it.

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:23.840
So this has basically changed
it for the entire company.

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:28.480
And as a super admin who
has all the privileges,

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:35.960
I can go back to
reporting and check

00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:38.360
on admin audit, which
basically gives you

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:41.810
a full list of all the
actions that admins are doing.

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.750
So there I see that
Shivani, who's the admin,

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:48.620
has gone and changed the sharing
outside the domain setting

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:53.480
and removed the warning while
the sharing is happening.

00:33:53.480 --> 00:33:56.690
And at the same time,
I've also got an alert

00:33:56.690 --> 00:34:00.050
saying that this setting
has been changed by Shivani.

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:02.220
I'm not happy about it,
because I'm the super admin.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:04.310
and this has weakened the
security of my company.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:08.000
Because she has gone ahead
and removed the warning

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:10.350
from users when they're
sharing outside of the domain.

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:20.150
So I can simply go back, look
for her username in the admin

00:34:20.150 --> 00:34:27.530
console, and I see one role
has been assigned to her.

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:34.489
And I say manage roles, and
I uncheck the services admin

00:34:34.489 --> 00:34:36.199
role, and I say, update.

00:34:36.199 --> 00:34:38.150
Because I feel she needs
some more training,

00:34:38.150 --> 00:34:40.429
so let's remove her
roles for the time being,

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:41.960
and she can go
back, get trained,

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:44.870
and then we can trust
her with admin assignment

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:46.679
after this is done.

00:34:46.679 --> 00:34:49.489
So this is how super-admins
have a very powerful control

00:34:49.489 --> 00:34:51.710
through admin audit,
because admin audit keeps

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:53.715
a close watch on
admin activities

00:34:53.715 --> 00:34:55.590
that are happening not
just on admin console,

00:34:55.590 --> 00:34:57.030
but even using API.

00:34:57.030 --> 00:34:59.310
It's a very powerful
mechanism to keep in control.

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:03.370
So with that, I'll just
hand it back to Allan.

00:35:03.370 --> 00:35:04.370
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Great.

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:05.120
Thanks, Rishi.

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:08.030
And so that's given
you a high level

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:11.600
look at some key capabilities
in the admin console

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:12.410
around reporting.

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:14.096
Now there's an awful
lot more there,

00:35:14.096 --> 00:35:15.470
but we picked a
few examples that

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:17.330
really highlight some
of the key things

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:19.670
that you want to focus in on.

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:21.590
However we talked
earlier that that

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:24.420
isn't the extent of our
reporting and analysis

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:25.350
strategy.

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:29.290
We also want to ensure that
we've got good integrations Via

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:30.350
an API.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:35.000
And the one that we're really
excited about is BigQuery.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:37.490
And we're really excited about
that because of the power

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:39.530
and the flexibility of BigQuery.

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:42.530
Our large customers have custom
and advanced requirements

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:44.540
that we couldn't
hope to possibly meet

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:46.550
in a single console.

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:49.040
They've got specific
compliance needs,

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:51.166
they've got risk,
legal assessments

00:35:51.166 --> 00:35:52.040
that they need to do.

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:55.100
They also want to mix data
from multiple sources.

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:57.860
And very importantly,
they want to be

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.950
able to spread the insights
out to targeted individuals

00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:03.440
in their company, not just
to delegated admins, right?

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:05.690
They want to syndicate
out this information

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:07.550
and they want to really,
really control it.

00:36:07.550 --> 00:36:10.150
And that's where
BigQuery comes in.

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:13.180
We've got this connection
between or admin reports

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:16.076
and our admin data, and it's
able to flow into BigQuery.

00:36:16.076 --> 00:36:17.450
And what Rishi is
going to do, is

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:20.360
he's going to walk you through
a few different scenarios that

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:23.360
are showing just the amazing
slice and dice that you've

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:26.520
got in BigQuery, and also
the really, really powerful

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:28.820
and, to be honest, very
beautiful visualization

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:30.620
that is present in
Data Studio that's

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:32.070
connected in there as well.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:34.370
So Rishi, I'll hand
over to you, and you

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:35.570
can run us through that.

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:35.994
RISHI DHAND: Sure.

00:36:35.994 --> 00:36:36.494
Thanks, Al.

00:36:36.494 --> 00:36:37.855
ALLAN LIVINGSTON: Thank you.

00:36:37.855 --> 00:36:39.570
RISHI DHAND: So I
just want to highlight

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:42.090
with BigQuery integration,
what we are offering is,

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:46.880
admins can now directly export
data from their admin console

00:36:46.880 --> 00:36:47.720
to BigQuery.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:50.210
And this is really important
because it cuts down

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:54.770
the entire effort to build an
application, to call our APIs,

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:56.910
and then from API, put
the data into BigQuery.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:59.750
So this is a direct
route that admins have.

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:02.930
And just to tell you a little
bit about the feature itself.

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:05.240
It's secure, because when
you go to admin console,

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:08.870
and it looks at the signed
in admin's credentials

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:13.220
and only shows those BigQuery
projects where this admin has

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:14.210
editable rights on.

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:18.230
So that he cannot take this data
to any other BigQuery project.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:21.710
It is pretty scalable, and
its one check enablement,

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:24.590
so the configuration
is pretty lightweight.

00:37:24.590 --> 00:37:27.200
And after this push
is done, admins

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:29.870
can use BigQuery to write
scalable custom queries,

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:33.140
or they can create their own
reports using Data Studio.

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:39.210
So I will quickly show you
what this report looks like.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:46.524
So let's see one such report.

00:37:46.524 --> 00:37:47.190
So there you go.

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:49.640
So there's this
report, which admin

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:51.240
can create for collaboration.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:53.720
So this shows how different
users inside the company

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:56.870
are collaborating across
different applications.

00:37:56.870 --> 00:38:00.500
Very beautiful, very custom,
and it's very easy to build.

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:04.550
Similar to this, I
can do another report,

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:07.280
which shows you a comparison
between different OUs.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.010
So this is a classic
CIO staff, where

00:38:10.010 --> 00:38:12.800
you want to compare different
teams, how different teams are

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:15.150
using different Google products.

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:20.430
And I can customize this
by choosing specific OUs.

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:22.124
So NA is North America.

00:38:22.124 --> 00:38:23.540
I choose North
America, and I want

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:27.410
to compare that with email.

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:28.110
So there you go.

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:31.130
So as a CIO I'm
able to compare how

00:38:31.130 --> 00:38:33.290
different teams
inside my company

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:39.320
are able to compare usage across
different Google products.

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:42.830
There is also another
important report

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.850
that I can create
around security.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:49.190
So this report shows me
how files are being shared

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:50.930
broken down by visibility.

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:54.430
So whether it's external,
outside the domain, internal--

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:56.400
Don't and I can break it down.

00:38:56.400 --> 00:39:00.310
And I can run some more
filters on top of this.

00:39:00.310 --> 00:39:02.780
For example, if I just want
to see public in the domain.

00:39:02.780 --> 00:39:07.010
I can click on this, and it
shows me public in the domain.

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:10.490
And then I can apply
further filters

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:11.600
to get more of this thing.

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:13.340
More granularity.

00:39:13.340 --> 00:39:16.880
Now if I want to
edit this report,

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:23.340
and say, add a new
page, I can simply

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:28.140
choose one of the
available widgets.

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:31.170
I can break it down.

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:43.700
There you go.

00:39:43.700 --> 00:39:46.390
So I was able to create exact
same report right in front

00:39:46.390 --> 00:39:58.060
of you I can also
select a filter,

00:39:58.060 --> 00:40:01.890
choose the visibility type.

00:40:05.842 --> 00:40:06.830
There you go.

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:10.130
So I just created a
report in few seconds,

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:14.180
showing me how files are getting
shared outside my company.

00:40:14.180 --> 00:40:15.600
And that's the
power of BigQuery.

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:17.020
Once you have the
data out there,

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:19.040
you can connect
it to Data Studio,

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:21.380
run visualizations,
and customize it

00:40:21.380 --> 00:40:22.850
in a matter of seconds.

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:25.460
And that's what makes this
feature very, very exciting

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:27.380
and very powerful.

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:28.990
With this, I'll
hand it back to Al.

